		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Sequential Impulses Causing To Much Bounce]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=2758#p2758"><![CDATA[
Some extra restitution/bounce/energy might be introduced by penetration depth recovery. This is a general problem, not particular to sequential impulse method. One solution is to introduce continuous collision detection and proceed to the earliest time of impact, so no penetration is ever introduced. This is very complex, so the following option is easier to achieve:<br><br>You can prevent extra velocity created due to penetration depth recovery, by splitting the normal impulse from the penetration impulse. See Erin's posting about 'split impulse for more details here:<br><a href="http://continuousphysics.com/Bullet/phpBB2/viewtopic.php?p=2121&amp;highlight=#2121" class="postlink">http://continuousphysics.com/Bullet/php ... ight=#2121</a><br><br>Erwin<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=2">Erwin Coumans</a> — Sat Dec 09, 2006 6:23 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
